Q: Data shared across screens being emptied every time screen popup A: <p>think I made a mistake here. I was defining <code>&lt;ItemProvider&gt;</code> twice respectively in <code>app/_layout</code> and <code>app/menu/_layout</code>, make it serve different contexts for both <code>&lt;Stack&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Slot&gt;</code>. For the MenuLayout I should just return <code>&lt;Slot&gt;</code> directly.</p>
<pre class="lang-tsx prettyprint-override"><code>export default function MenuLayout() {
  return (&lt;Slot/&gt;);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Layout and visual animations out of sync A: <h3>1. The Core Issue: Grid Flow vs. Transforms</h3>
<p>In a CSS Grid, every child takes up a row. If you have 12 rows and you use <code>translateY(-900%)</code> on the 10th row, it moves to the top visually, but the grid still thinks there are 12 rows of space being occupied.</p>
<p>To solve this without breaking <code>subgrid</code>, you need to:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Keep the container <code>overflow: hidden</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Absolute Position the rows</strong> during the animation so they don't push the grid height out, OR (simpler) <strong>adjust the grid-row assignments</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. The Optimized Solution</h3>
<p>Instead of complex <code>translateY</code> math, you can use <code>grid-row</code> and <code>opacity</code>. By assigning all &quot;collapsed&quot; rows to the same grid-row index, they will physically overlap at the top of the table, allowing the container height to transition smoothly.</p>
<pre><code>.table {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: auto max-content;
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Use grid-template-rows to control row height strictly */
  grid-template-rows: 40px repeat(var(--total-items), 50px);
  height: calc(var(--number-of-rows) * 50px + 40px);
  transition: height 0.5s ease;
}

.table-row {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: subgrid;
  grid-column: span 2;
  height: 50px;
  /* Smoothly fade out rows that are being &quot;hidden&quot; */
  transition: transform 0.5s ease, opacity 0.3s ease;
}

/* When collapsed, move non-selected rows to an invisible state */
.table-row.hidden {
  opacity: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Recommended JavaScript Logic</p>
<p>Instead of calculating multipliers, simply toggle a class. Let the CSS handle the &quot;stacking.&quot;</p>
<pre><code>function updateTransforms() {
  const table = document.getElementById('table');
  const selectedIndex = state.items.findIndex(item =&gt; item.selected);

  // Logic: Show selected row + 2 rows below it (or above if at the end)
  let visibleIndices = [selectedIndex, selectedIndex + 1, selectedIndex + 2];

  // Adjust if we are at the end of the list
  if (selectedIndex &gt;= state.items.length - 2) {
    visibleIndices = [state.items.length - 3, state.items.length - 2, state.items.length - 1];
  }

  state.items.forEach((item, index) =&gt; {
    if (isOpen) {
      item.element.style.transform = `translateY(0)`;
      item.element.style.opacity = &quot;1&quot;;
    } else {
      if (visibleIndices.includes(index)) {
        // Move the visible rows to the top 3 slots
        const targetPos = visibleIndices.indexOf(index);
        const moveDistance = (index - targetPos) * 50; // 50px is row height
        item.element.style.transform = `translateY(-${moveDistance}px)`;
        item.element.style.opacity = &quot;1&quot;;
      } else {
        item.element.style.opacity = &quot;0&quot;;
      }
    }
  });

  const totalRows = isOpen ? state.items.length : 3;
  table.style.setProperty('--number-of-rows', totalRows);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to detect if there&#39;s a Windows Update available using Delphi code? A: <p>There is a COM object in Windows, named <code>Microsoft.Update.Session</code>, that you can query, with parameters for online/offline check (and more).</p>
<p>Here is a function that checks for updates (either a full online check, or just an offline check of whether some updates are already pending), and optionally also fills in a string list with the available update names:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>uses 
  System.Classes,
  System.SysUtils,
  Winapi.ActiveX,
  System.Win.ComObj,
  System.Variants;

// Meaning of the WithOnlineCheck parameter:
//   False = do NOT go online, 
//   True = check online (may take long time)
// UpdateInfo, if passed in, gets filled
// with the titles of the available updates
function AreWindowsUpdatesAvailable(WithOnlineCheck: Boolean;
  UpdateInfo: TStrings = nil): Boolean;
var
  Criteria: string;
  UpdateSession: OleVariant;  // Microsoft.Update.Session
  UpdateSearcher: OleVariant;  // IUpdateSearcher
  SearchResult: OleVariant;  // ISearchResult
  Update: OleVariant;  // IUpdate
  i: Integer;
begin
  // These criteria mirror what the Windows &quot;Settings&quot; app UI would show
  Criteria := 'IsInstalled=0 and IsHidden=0 and IsAssigned=1';

  UpdateSession := CreateOleObject('Microsoft.Update.Session');

  // (Optional) client ID
  UpdateSession.ClientApplicationID := 'Delphi application check';

  UpdateSearcher := UpdateSession.CreateUpdateSearcher;
  UpdateSearcher.Online := WithOnlineCheck; 

  SearchResult := UpdateSearcher.Search(Criteria);

  Result := (not VarIsNull(SearchResult)) and 
            (SearchResult.Updates.Count &gt; 0);

  // Optionally return the titles of the updates
  if Result and (UpdateInfo &lt;&gt; nil) then
  begin
    UpdateInfo.Clear;
    for i := 0 to SearchResult.Updates.Count - 1 do
    begin
      Update := SearchResult.Updates[i];
      UpdateInfo.Add(Update.Title);
    end;
  end;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>Remember to call <code>CoInitialize(nil)</code> somewhere at startup of the program (for example in some <code>initialization</code> section) and <code>CoUninitialize</code> when the program closes (for example in the corresponding <code>finalization</code> section), unless the program is a VCL forms application (VCL forms applications make these calls for you automatically, but not VCL service applications and not console applications).</p>

Q: Is a Kotlin coroutine Job&#39;s status reliable for concurrency control? A: <p>Checking on <code>isActive</code> is fine in Kotlin, as the example code from the official documentation shows - no reason to doubt that.</p>
<p>Another option is to use <code>ensureActive()</code>, which internally tests for <code>isActive</code> and (if false) aborts the current coroutine right then and there by throwing a CancellationException. That is the mechanism how coroutines in Kotlin communicate their status to other nodes in the coroutine hierarchy. The <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.coroutines/kotlinx-coroutines-core/kotlinx.coroutines/ensure-active.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">documentation</a> provides this example:</p>
<pre><code>while (true) {
    // Repeatatively do some background work that is non-suspending
    backgroundWork()
    ensureActive() // Bail out if the scope was cancelled
    postBackgroundCleanup() // Won't be invoked if the scope was cancelled
}
</code></pre>
